Q: In a Win UI 3 app, if the restore button is disabled and the app is minimized then reopened, the UI moves down. What could cause this? A: <p>Not sure if this is by design but, it looks like on the second maximizing (minimize -&gt; restore), the task bar height is ignored, ending up with a longer window height. This is why the window content is shifted down.
Just removing <code>IsMaximizable = false</code> seems avoid this weird behavior.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>if (e.SourcePageType == typeof(SecondPage))
{
    // On second page: maximize and disable resizing
    presenter.Maximize();
    //presenter.IsMaximizable = false;
    presenter.IsResizable = false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you need <code>IsMaximizable = false</code> anyway, you might need file an issue on the WinUI repo and wait until it gets fixed.</p>

Q: Facing CORS issue in .NET 4.5 and Angular application A: <p>You tried alot of different things in an attempt to enable CORS but failed to see one thing that is preventing the CORS preflight requests from succeeding.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>protected void Application_BeginRequest(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (HttpContext.Current.Request.HttpMethod == &quot;OPTIONS&quot;)
    {
        HttpContext.Current.Response.StatusCode = 405;
        HttpContext.Current.Response.End();
    }

    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>You're going to need to remove this check for OPTIONS requests and allow them.</p>
<p>CORS sends a preflight (OPTIONS) request that needs to be successful before the actual request is sent. A failure during the preflight request will cause the CORS error you're seeing.</p>
<p>Have a read of <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)</a> over on <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MDN</a> for more information about the details of how CORS works.</p>
